description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/memory.rs:382-430 query_objects takes _filter and _sort as ignored parameters. The result is the SAME id list regardless of any filter the caller passes. The integration test at tests/query_changes_tests.rs:73-262 introduces an entire FilteringBackend wrapper just so accountIds filtering can be tested.

Maintainer burden scenario:
  1. A downstream implementor of SharingBackend reads memory.rs as the reference. They see query_objects returning a sorted+paginated id slice with no filter evaluation. They reasonably conclude that 'filter evaluation is handled upstream by the dispatcher' or 'filters are advisory'.
  2. Their backend ignores filter too.
  3. A client passes filter={'type':'individual'}; the response includes groups, locations, and resources. The client treats this as a server bug.
  4. The implementor opens an issue against this crate asking why the reference backend doesn't honor filters. The repeating class of user reports begins here.

The module-level docstring at memory.rs:15-17 says 'a number of RFC 9670 edge cases are simplified (see source comments)' which is supposed to cover this — but the only inline comment near query_objects is a one-line // Apply JSON Merge Patch elsewhere. There is no rustdoc on query_objects saying 'this implementation IGNORES filter and sort; production backends MUST evaluate them.'

Fix: add a prominent rustdoc on query_objects (and query_changes which delegates to get_changes for the same reason):

    /// **Reference-only**: this implementation ignores  and .
    /// Production backends MUST evaluate the filter expression and apply
    /// the comparators per RFC 8620 §5.5. The crate's integration tests
    /// wrap this backend in a  (see
    /// ) when filter evaluation is required.

Same shape exists in 6 sibling MemoryBackends — propagation needed.
